    <h1>Sanying vs Zidantou: Which High-Heat Chilli to Choose</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Sanying and Zidantou are the two classic hot-sauce varieties at 20,000–50,000 SHU — the same heat band, but different pods, different origins, and different powder behaviour. This comparison separates them so you order the right one.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Sanying (Tianying) is a cluster-type facing-heaven chilli from Henan and Hebei; Zidantou is a conical bullet-shaped chilli from Shandong and Anhui with thicker flesh. Both are 20,000–50,000 SHU hot-sauce varieties, but Zidantou's higher flesh-to-skin ratio grinds into more uniform powder.
    </div>
    <AtAGlance facts={glanceFacts} />

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Sanying (Tianying)</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Zidantou (bullet)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Shape</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Cluster-type facing-heaven</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Conical bullet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Region</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Henan, Hebei</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Shandong, Anhui</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">20,000–50,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">20,000–50,000</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Flesh</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Thin-walled cluster</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Thick flesh, higher flesh-to-skin ratio</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Grind</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Classic dry-chilli shape</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">More uniform powder, less seed &amp; skin variation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Best for</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Hot sauce, chilli oil, snack seasoning</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Hot sauce, industrial food, chilli powder</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the choice matters</h2>
    <p>
      Both are the classic hot-sauce varieties at 20,000–50,000 SHU — see the <a href="/heat-levels/high-heat/">high heat</a> page. The difference is the pod. <a href="/products/sanying-chilli/">Sanying</a> is a cluster-type facing-heaven chilli from Henan and Hebei, the dependable high-heat base for hot sauce, chilli oil and snack seasoning. <a href="/products/zidantou-chilli/">Zidantou</a> is a conical bullet from Shandong and Anhui whose thick flesh grinds into a robust, consistent powder — the reason it is the dependable high-heat powder base for hot sauce and industrial food.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When to choose each</h2>
    <p>
      Choose Sanying when the recipe is built on the classic facing-heaven shape and dried whole pods; choose Zidantou when the recipe is powder-based and grind uniformity matters. They substitute for heat inside the same SHU band, but not for shape and flesh ratio.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety and a precise SHU window. Heat, colour and flavour vary independently, so the variety name is what locks batch-to-batch consistency.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat and colour values are representative. Confirm your exact SHU window, ASTA, form and mesh
      when you inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between Sanying and Zidantou?</h3>
      <p>Sanying (Tianying) is a cluster-type facing-heaven chilli from Henan and Hebei; Zidantou is a conical bullet-shaped chilli from Shandong and Anhui with thicker flesh. Both are 20,000–50,000 SHU hot-sauce varieties, but Zidantou's higher flesh-to-skin ratio grinds into more uniform powder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for hot sauce?</h3>
      <p>Both are classic hot-sauce varieties in the same 20,000–50,000 SHU band. Sanying is the dependable high-heat base; Zidantou is preferred where the recipe is powder-based and grind uniformity matters.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Are Sanying and Zidantou interchangeable?</h3>
      <p>For heat, yes — they sit in the same SHU band. For shape and powder behaviour, no: the pod shapes and flesh ratios differ. Confirm the variety and a precise SHU window in your specification.</p>
    </div>

